    "body": "- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\r\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\r\n\r\n[Curly and colored underline support isn't standard and isn't commonly supported by many terminal emulators (and programs)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ANSI_escape_code#SGR_(Select_Graphic_Rendition)_parameters), so if one were to view some spelling mistakes with a color scheme generated by mini.base16 or mini.hues in a terminal emulator other than a select few (albeit still common, like xterm, urxvt, st, etc.), plain underlines would be shown instead of curly and colored ones. This can cause confusion with other sources of underlines, like mini.cursorword, and it can be difficult to see and differentiate (bad spelling, bad capitilization, local words, rare words). This is what it could look like on such terminal: \r\n![screenshot](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/assets/50467323/c5efb44f-f635-4655-9c6e-56f4d9332e91)\r\nThis PR aims to eliminate this problem by doing what many other (Neo)Vim color schemes do: change the foreground color as well. It will instead maybe look like this, but it's still color scheme dependent:\r\n![other](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/assets/50467323/34f25904-a8a6-4109-9543-936d6ce8e0e2)",
    "closed_at": "2024-01-04T09:04:10Z",
    "comment_data": [],
    "comment_regular_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the PR!\r\n\r\nHaving both undercurl and foreground colored is a bit too much. Although not universal, support for colored undercurl is still quite common while fallback to colored underline is still usable (albeit barely).\r\n\r\nThe suggested way for users to tweak highlight groups is to define them manually after enabling color scheme (ideally inside autocommand for `ColorScheme` event).\r\n\r\nClosing as not planned.",
            "created_at": "2024-01-04T09:04:10Z",
